WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:12.340
In the 1953 science fiction film The Beast
from 20,000 Fathoms, a giant monster attacks

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:15.930
New York City — as giant monsters are known
to do.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:21.820
While the film’s plot isn’t particularly
noteworthy, one element that stands out comes

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:27.310
about halfway through the film, when a group
of scientists try to figure out the evolutionary

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.539
history of the mysterious creature.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:36.490
Our interest in the theoretical biology of
unknown lifeforms hasn’t gone anywhere.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:41.460
For the past century, stories of cryptids
— like Bigfoot or the Loch Ness monster

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.440
— have continued to fascinate.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:49.520
And while these creatures likely don’t exist,
that doesn’t mean it isn’t fun to speculate

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.190
on their biology.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:59.030
Enter Cryptozoologicon, a book that imagines
evolutionary backstories for legendary creatures.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:04.239
Created by renowned paleontologist Darren
Naish, alongside channel mainstays C.M.

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:12.869
Koseman and John Conway — the volume is
so detailed that I had to know more: The Archivist:

00:01:12.869 --> 00:01:18.600
“So, where did the idea of writing a book
on the speculative biology of cryptids come

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.600
from?”

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.700
Darren Naish: “The idea did originate with
me.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.170
I said, ‘well, there’s all these creatures
in cryptozoology that are posited as potentially

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:32.750
real animals, but are essentially exercises
in speculative zoology.’

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:38.210
The cryptozoological literature has got this
extraordinary amount of speculation about:

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:43.250
if these creatures are real, what are they
like, what’s their evolutionary history?”

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:50.180
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
investigate the imagined biology of the world’s

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.969
most famous cryptids.

00:01:51.969 --> 00:01:56.830
You can purchase the book and follow and support
the creators using the links in the description

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:57.830
below.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:03.730
Now, let’s explore the creatures of Cryptozoologicon…

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:10.640
We’ll start with the celebrities of the
cryptid world — the hairy humanoids best

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.390
known from their alleged footprints.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.130
Darren Naish: “Obviously the most familiar
cryptids are Bigfoot, Yeti.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:20.180
Every culture in the world that we know about
has legends of wild people of some kind.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:24.360
I think there’s a good case to be made that
that’s a human-wide expectation I think

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.900
that’s already embedded within our cultural
landscape.”

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.519
Bigfoot and the Yeti may be a socio-cultural
phenomenon rather an undiscovered primate

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:38.940
species, but if we were to envision their
evolutionary history, an interesting challenge

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:39.940
emerges.

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:44.940
Just how do these speculative hominids relate
to us — and to each other?

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:50.710
The Cryptozoologicon team’s solution is
to imagine Bigfoot and the Yeti as large,

00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:55.129
herbivorous apes, more closely related to
orangutans than humans.

00:02:55.129 --> 00:03:01.080
The project imagines the Yeti and Bigfoot
to be close cousins, related sub-species possessing

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.739
different adaptations for their respective
environments.

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:10.629
In fact, the Cryptozoologicon team created
an entire phylogenetic tree to show how these

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:16.799
lineages connect not just to each other, but
to other, lesser-known ape-cryptids from around

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:18.000
the world.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.500
The Orang Pendek, for example, are smaller,
red-coated humanoids from Sumatran folklore,

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:30.110
which the team pictures as another member
of this forgotten branch of the primate family

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.110
tree.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:37.700
But this is just scratching the surface of
the team’s creativity…

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:43.560
Another famous yet far more unusual cryptid
comes from the folklore of the Americas.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:49.879
The Chupacabra is a fabled creature said to
drain the blood from livestock like goats.

00:03:49.879 --> 00:03:55.740
What exactly the Chupacabra is supposed to
look like varies a lot between alleged eyewitness

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:01.939
descriptions — from a dog-like creature,
to more of a bloodthirsty kangaroo.

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:07.689
If the Chupacabra were a real animal, the
team imagines it would be a semi-bipedal,

00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:09.270
nocturnal predator.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:15.930
Yet unexpectedly, the team pictures the Chupacabra
as a giant relative of the opossum.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:21.000
The opossum lineage might not seem like the
one most likely to produce a bloodthirsty

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:27.500
terror of the night, but in fact the strong
jaws and formidable upper canines of opossums

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:33.490
would require little evolutionary modification
to produce a large-bodied predator.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:38.720
If such a creature did roam the Americas,
one would have to be extra careful after the

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.180
sun went down...

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:48.570
When we think about reptilian beasts lurking
below the water, many of us might think of

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:50.400
the Loch Ness Monster.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:56.870
But off the coast of New Zealand in 1977,
a Japanese fishing trawler reportedly found

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:58.510
something else.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:04.900
The Zuiyo Maru Carcass was a corpse the fishing
crew accidently hauled in from the deep sea,

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.430
which many speculated was some kind of sea
monster.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:15.030
Thorough analysis of amino acids in the corpse's
muscle tissue later indicated it was in fact

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:20.070
the carcass of a basking shark, which had
decomposed in a peculiar manner.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:21.470
But what if that wasn’t the case?

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:27.660
The Archivist: “So, what was the team’s
thought process behind reimagining the Zuiyo

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.660
Maru Creature?”

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:34.450
Darren Naish: “We are thinking, if we run
with this Zuiyo Maru Creature as if it’s

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:38.560
an actual cryptid and a new kind of animal,
then what would it look like?

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.720
And the carcass, of course, is a decomposing
carcass, it’s not pretty, it’s got like

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.220
strands of decomposing flesh hanging off it.

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:50.590
So we thought: wouldn’t it be fun if that
was the creature’s real appearance?

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:56.020
It’s some kind of like — hideous, zombie,
deep water creature with tassels and strands

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:57.020
hanging off its body.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:06:02.180
The sort of thing that we do know of in the
real world thanks to peculiar deep-sea fishes.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:04.990
You know, deep sea angler fishes and what
not.”

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:09.720
The team goes on to speculate that perhaps
the lumps of the Zuiyo Maru Creature could

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:15.500
help it mimic the lumpy, muddy terrain of
the sea floor — or even mimic a rotted corpse

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.440
to appear less appetizing to predators.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:23.060
In either case, it’s fun to speculate on
— even if the real explanation behind the

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:27.979
carcass is less spectacular...

00:06:27.979 --> 00:06:34.039
According to Aboriginal mythology, the wilderness
of south-eastern Australia is home to a fearsome

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:36.030
creature known as the Bunyip.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:42.569
An amorphous water monster, the Bunyip is
often described as a flippered, amphibious

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:44.690
animal that attacks from the water.

00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:49.639
While some depictions of the Bunyip are quite
fantastical, others are more grounded, to

00:06:49.639 --> 00:06:55.380
the point that some have theorized that the
Bunyip might be a cultural memory of a large,

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:59.250
extinct Australian marsupial, of which there
are several.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:04.590
Other theories suggest that the Bunyip actually
comes from sightings of animals like the Platypus

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:08.569
— which frankly does look quite a bit like
a mythical creature.

00:07:08.569 --> 00:07:13.900
But if the Bunyip were an undiscovered animal,
the team’s concept for the creature is a

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.280
semi-aquatic marsupial.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:21.879
The idea of an aquatic marsupial presents
an interesting challenge, as marsupials give

00:07:21.879 --> 00:07:25.599
birth to under-developed babies that they
raise in a pouch.

00:07:25.599 --> 00:07:32.160
As a solution, the team proposes that like
the unusual South American water possum, the

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:35.150
Bunyip’s pouch has a water-tight seal.

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:40.360
Such an adaptation could allow the Bunyip
to thrive in aquatic environments — something

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:45.910
that real Australian marsupials have struggled
to do…

00:07:45.910 --> 00:07:51.789
The tale of the Beast of Gévaudan is one
of the most genuinely interesting and intriguing

00:07:51.789 --> 00:07:54.600
of cryptozoological mysteries.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:08:03.380
Between 1764 and 1767 in south-eastern France,
over 70 people seemingly perished from encounters

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:06.410
with an enormous, wolf-like beast.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:12.090
The beast quickly became legendary, and multiple
large wolves believed to be responsible for

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.330
the Beast’s attacks were slain during that
time.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:20.409
Allegedly, a rogue Hyena was eventually caught
and killed as well.

00:08:20.409 --> 00:08:26.620
As unlikely as that might sound, the animal’s
pelt was documented going on display in Paris

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:32.640
years later — confirming that an escaped
hyena really did briefly roam the French countryside.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.800
In view of all this, it seems the attacks
came from numerous culprits, the stories of

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:41.240
which were inadvertently combined to form
the legend.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:48.060
But now, let’s imagine that the Beast of
Gévaudan was a single, undiscovered monster.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:53.779
Taking inspiration from reports that the beast
had curved claws and a long, slender tail,

00:08:53.779 --> 00:08:59.380
the team interprets the creature not as a
wolf or Hyena, but a type of giant mustelid.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:05.730
Mustelids are a family of carnivorous mammals
that includes otters, ferrets, and wolverines.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:11.980
This version of the Beast of Gévaudan would
be an entirely new subset of the mustelid

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:20.339
family, and would make the European countryside
far more dangerous...

00:09:20.339 --> 00:09:26.970
Thanks in part to a somewhat notorious faux-documentary
that aired on the Discovery Channel in 2013,

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:32.930
the idea that a certain bus-sized prehistoric
shark could be living undetected has become

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:34.410
a popular one.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:40.190
The Archivist: “Given your expertise as
a paleontologist, just how outlandish is it

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:43.070
that the Megaladon survived into the modern
era?”

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:45.610
Darren Naish: “There’s no chance that
it’s still alive.

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:49.930
It’s not really appreciated how poor the
case is.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:55.709
Lots of data on the position of megalodon
teeth in the fossil record has shown that

00:09:55.709 --> 00:10:01.430
this shark did not survive into the Holocene
— the modern era — and it probably didn’t

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.010
even survive into the Pleistocene.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:07.220
Most of them have turned out to be older than
about two million years old.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:10.070
Way, way, way before the modern era.”

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:16.330
Yet in a speculative parallel universe where
the Megalodon did still swim the seas, what

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:17.700
would it look like?

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:23.040
In order for a whale-eating shark to remain
elusive, it would likely have to have evolved

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:26.370
into a deepwater fish that lurks in undersea
canyons.

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:33.460
The team suggests that this new, abyssal Megalodon
would be a ghostly white, and would be a separate

00:10:33.460 --> 00:10:36.959
species from its Pliocene ancestors.

00:10:36.959 --> 00:10:44.430
Such terrors of the deep would certainly make
swimming more nerve-wracking…

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:50.190
On the Indonesian island of Java, a demon
is said to fly through the mountains on leathery

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:51.310
wings.

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:57.310
Infrequent reports from the 1920s describe
the Ahool, a bat-like terror with a massive

00:10:57.310 --> 00:11:00.310
wingspan and the face of a primate.

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:06.019
While it’s unlikely such an organism ever
existed, the concept of a giant bat monster

00:11:06.019 --> 00:11:11.350
isn’t too hard to reimagine as something
more biologically plausible.

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:17.269
The team envisions the Ahool as an unknown
bat-lineage even larger than the fox-faced

00:11:17.269 --> 00:11:18.269
megabats.

00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:23.670
A burly predator, the Ahool is able to walk
bipedally on the ground — bringing prey

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:27.250
to the forest floor after tackling them in
the air.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:33.230
An aggressive carnivore, this creature possesses
a short snout that vaguely resembles the face

00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:34.840
of a monkey.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:41.440
All in all, it’s an astute reimagining of
a rather unlikely lifeform...

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:47.200
According to folklore, hidden in the misty
moors of Scotland lurks a spectral horse.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:53.610
‘Kelpie’ is an old Scottish name for an
equine monster said to dwell within deep lakes,

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:58.250
hoping to drag unsuspecting travelers to a
watery grave.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:03.470
Since horses aren’t amphibious, the Kelpie
is a challenging creature to reimagine as

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:05.329
a plausible lifeform.

00:12:05.329 --> 00:12:10.340
But there are several hoofed mammal species,
like wildebeest or water buffalo, that do

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:13.400
routinely wallow in rivers and lakes.

00:12:13.400 --> 00:12:19.380
More surprisingly, there are even some species
of chevrotain — bizarre animals also known

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:24.490
as ‘mouse-deer’ — that are known to
walk underwater along the bottom of pools.

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:30.730
So, the team imagines the Kelpie as giant
relatives of the chevrotain that possess thick

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:36.959
manes and cloven hooves that make them look
superficially horse-like from a distance.

00:12:36.959 --> 00:12:42.440
Reimagining Kelpies as semi-aquatic chevrotains
could also explain their occasional carnivorous

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:46.740
behavior from the legends, as chevrotains
are omnivores.

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:52.520
The team imagines the Kelpie possess fang-like
teeth to drag prey into the water — which

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:58.339
would, on occasion, includes humans.

00:12:58.339 --> 00:13:04.079
In the Likouala River region of the Congo
basin, a reptile-like creature with plates

00:13:04.079 --> 00:13:07.149
on its back supposedly lurks in the swamp.

00:13:07.149 --> 00:13:12.959
Upon hearing of this creature’s description,
some outsiders interpreted the beast — called

00:13:12.959 --> 00:13:19.019
the Mbielu-Mbielu-Mbielu — as some kind
of aquatic stegosaur that had miraculously

00:13:19.019 --> 00:13:21.970
survived the KT extinction event.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:27.250
While this would be cool, it’s… quite
the leap to make, and even for their speculative

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:31.360
reimagining, the team found the stegosaur
explanation to be too far-fetched.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:36.670
Darren Naish: “If you’re going to take
seriously that this is a real animal, then

00:13:36.670 --> 00:13:40.890
stegosaur is way down the list of things you
could classify as probable.

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:46.630
And so, for Cryptozoologicon we thought, wait
a minute, there’s this group of relatively

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:54.700
large tropical African fish that have some
amphibious capability, and have dorsal finlets

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:58.580
forming, like, a single line along the top
of the body; they’re called the bichirs.

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.579
So, let’s have some fun with this.

00:14:00.579 --> 00:14:07.660
What about the idea that Mbielu-Mbielu-Mbielu
is a gigantic, amphibious, unusual bichir.”

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:13.810
The team imagines that this speculative lifeform
uses its massive spines to send signals to

00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:19.950
other members of its kind, and can clamber
onto land using its muscular front flippers.

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:25.560
It’s still a pretty extreme concept for
an animal — but less so than an asteroid-proof

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:30.570
aquatic stegosaurus.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:37.310
Wherever a dark street winds through the countryside
in the United States, the Goatman is supposedly

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:38.880
on the prowl.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:44.470
As the jokey nature of the name might suggest,
the Goatman is for the most part not taken

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:49.550
seriously, and is just meant to be a fun yet
spooky urban legend.

00:14:49.550 --> 00:14:55.040
Exactly what the Goatman is and what it looks
like varies between stories, and is generally

00:14:55.040 --> 00:15:00.509
just meant to be a vaguely-humanoid creature
that prowls around in the dark.

00:15:00.509 --> 00:15:06.660
But playing the speculative game once more,
the team redesigns the Goatman as a large,

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:11.880
human-sized, North American monkey, uniquely
adapted for rapid bipedal running.

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:17.950
They picture these creatures as highly elusive,
able to rapidly sprint away from danger — hence

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:20.050
the confusion over their appearance.

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:25.089
It would certainly be a shock to encounter
such creatures, especially since the team

00:15:25.089 --> 00:15:33.160
imagines they’d be capable of ferocious
aggression when the need arises...

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:39.000
In the mountains of New Guinea, a report from
the 1930s describes a creature from another

00:15:39.000 --> 00:15:40.090
age.

00:15:40.090 --> 00:15:45.470
Stunt pilot and self-described adventurer
Charles Miller claims he witnessed a towering

00:15:45.470 --> 00:15:51.040
beast with a long neck and tail emerge from
the forest, which he believed to be a lost

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.190
Mesozoic dinosaur.

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:58.360
While the concept of dinosaurs surviving in
a lost valley somewhere was a bit of a cultural

00:15:58.360 --> 00:16:04.720
phenomenon at the time, the report wasn’t
believed, and years later Miller all but admitted

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:06.880
he made the encounter up.

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:12.589
If Miller’s monster was a real lifeform,
the Cryptozoologicon team imagines that like

00:16:12.589 --> 00:16:18.630
Mbielu-Mbielu-Mbielu it wouldn’t be a lost
dinosaur, but rather a dinosaur-like offshoot

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:20.250
of a modern genus.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:26.380
Taking inspiration from the Galapagos tortoise,
they picture the creature to be a turtle offshoot

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:32.150
that evolved into a giraffe-like herbivore
in an isolated island environment.

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:39.920
While not quite a dinosaur, a giant, super-necked
tortoise would be a sight to behold…

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:45.360
Arguably one of the most fascinating episodes
in cryptozoological history comes from the

00:16:45.360 --> 00:16:48.850
Colombian-Venezuelan border in 1929.

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:55.980
Here, Swiss geologist François De Loys took
a creepy photo of a deceased primate he claimed

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:58.510
was an undiscovered species.

00:16:58.510 --> 00:17:03.839
The infamous photo was treated with immediate
skepticism, and has been largely proven to

00:17:03.839 --> 00:17:08.679
be a white-faced spider monkey, that De Loys
propped up to make it appear larger than it

00:17:08.679 --> 00:17:09.919
actually was.

00:17:09.919 --> 00:17:13.629
But what if the De Loys Ape had been a real
animal?

00:17:13.629 --> 00:17:19.079
The team envisions a primate that split off
from spider monkeys during the Pleistocene,

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:21.720
becoming large, bipedal ground-dwellers.

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:27.839
In our timeline, this lineage includes some
of the most intelligent and adaptable primates,

00:17:27.839 --> 00:17:33.240
so it follows that the larger De Loys Ape
would be particularly smart, perhaps routinely

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:41.650
using tools and seeming almost human-like
when foraging and using weapons…

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:47.179
One of my absolute favorite aquatic cryptids
supposedly dwells in the waters of the north

00:17:47.179 --> 00:17:50.120
Atlantic — the ‘long-necked seal.’

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:56.010
Most reports come from the 18th century, and
describe seal-like creatures with peculiar,

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:57.870
elongated necks.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:02.970
The scarce sightings of this creature seem
to come from individuals who were unfamiliar

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:05.340
with the appearance of sea lions.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:11.559
The team envisions long-necked seals as a
genus of pinnipeds that fill a unique ecological

00:18:11.559 --> 00:18:12.559
niche.

00:18:12.559 --> 00:18:17.930
Instead of pursuing fish in the open water,
these creatures munch on slow moving sea cucumbers

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:20.830
and starfish on the abyssal plain.

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:25.840
Their nostrils have evolved into protruding
breathing snorkels — meaning that unlike

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:32.620
common seals, long necked seals never have
to come ashore…

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:38.070
Off the coast of Alaska, another aquatic cryptid
comes in the form of the Tizheruk.

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:43.830
A sleek aquatic monster, it’s possible this
creature comes from real sightings of leopard

00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:48.520
seals — although some legends describe it
as having horns.

00:18:48.520 --> 00:18:54.169
Faced with such a strange mixture of features,
the team has come up with an ingenious speculative

00:18:54.169 --> 00:18:55.169
solution.

00:18:55.169 --> 00:19:00.870
They imagine the Tizheruk to be extraordinarily
large pinnipeds — perhaps related to their

00:19:00.870 --> 00:19:03.039
version of long-necked seals.

00:19:03.039 --> 00:19:09.160
They envision this creature would eat small
marine mammals, and even the occasional human

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:10.160
swimmer.

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:15.660
Instead of horns, this Tizheruk possesses
two soft, retractable structures on the top

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:20.710
of its head, which they inflate when displaying
to potential mates or rivals.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:26.270
It’s an unusual feature, but when one is
trying to imagine a plausible version of a

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:28.860
seal with horns, one has to get creative...

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:36.800
And to conclude our experiment, one of the
most bizarre cryptids ever conceived comes

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:41.100
from the western United States in the form
of the Hoop Snake.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:47.049
This creature comes from tall tales in the
1700s, which describe a snake that can travel

00:19:47.049 --> 00:19:53.210
rapidly by grabbing its tail in its mouth
to form a hoop and roll like a living wheel.

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:58.280
Allegedly, Hoop Snakes would race across the
countryside in pursuit of prey.

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:03.180
As you can imagine, this sort of snake is
quite the challenge to redesign as a more

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:07.280
realistic creature — but not entirely impossible.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:13.419
Unlikely as it might sound, Golden Wheel Spiders
do curl themselves into a ball and roll down

00:20:13.419 --> 00:20:15.559
sand dunes to escape predators.

00:20:15.559 --> 00:20:20.830
They don't form hoops like the eponymous snake
- but they at least show that there is a precedent

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:25.490
for turning yourself into a round thing and
rolling away at high speeds.

00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:30.799
So, the team envisions a Hoop Snake that has
evolved in much the same way.

00:20:30.799 --> 00:20:36.570
The imagined creature’s musculature is specialized
around supporting a wheel-shape, and its sturdy

00:20:36.570 --> 00:20:39.700
bones are able to endure the bumps of the
road.

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:45.650
Furthermore, the interior of its mouth is
heavily keratinized and perfectly shaped to

00:20:45.650 --> 00:20:51.140
latch on to its tail, with a thick keratinous
flap sealing the tail in place.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:55.929
The western United States would certainly
be more perilous if deadly snakes could roll

00:20:55.929 --> 00:21:05.020
around at high speeds, but I still find myself
wishing I lived in a world with Hoop Snakes...

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:09.970
But according to Darren Naish himself, just
because a creature likely doesn’t exist,

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:14.740
it doesn’t mean it can’t tell us something
about ourselves and our cultures.

00:21:14.740 --> 00:21:19.309
Darren Naish: “I think Cryptozoology has
an enormous potential to teach us about how

00:21:19.309 --> 00:21:21.200
we behave.

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:28.020
People have these particular kinds of creatures
as sort of fixtures of the imagination.

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:34.571
You go to a big, deep, cold lake, you look
in the water, there’s automatically the

00:21:34.571 --> 00:21:37.830
idea — even if you see absolutely nothing
— there’s the idea that there could be

00:21:37.830 --> 00:21:41.020
some giant scary and predatory monster in
the lake.

00:21:41.020 --> 00:21:44.260
That’s a fixture of culture.

00:21:44.260 --> 00:21:48.809
Our species as a whole, we often seem to have
the same kinds of creatures.

00:21:48.809 --> 00:21:55.430
For me, Cryptozoology is culture — is firmly
embedded within cultures, whether those creatures

00:21:55.430 --> 00:21:57.360
exist or not.”

00:21:57.360 --> 00:22:02.360
A tremendous thank you to Darren Naish for
speaking to me about Cryptozoology.

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:08.029
Once again, you can purchase Cryptozoologicon
to see more reimagined cryptids and follow

00:22:08.029 --> 00:22:13.150
and support the creators using the links in
the description below.

00:22:13.150 --> 00:22:15.850
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:22:15.850 --> 00:22:20.900
